WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.483
Open the Fly screen and look at a Mars mission.

00:00:03.483 --> 00:00:05.484
Now look at a Moon mission.

00:00:05.484 --> 00:00:16.230
The two views look completely different — even though both spacecraft are doing the same thing: following an elliptical trajectory under gravity.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.046
The difference is the reference frame.

00:00:19.046 --> 00:00:21.789
Mars missions are drawn heliocentric.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:24.679
The Sun sits at the center of the view.

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:27.940
Earth and Mars trace their orbits around it.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:37.204
The spacecraft's arc is a piece of an ellipse around the Sun, with perihelion at Earth's distance and aphelion out near Mars.

00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:45.208
This frame makes the geometry legible — you can see the spacecraft catching up to Mars as Mars itself moves.

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:47.653
Moon missions are drawn cislunar.

00:00:47.653 --> 00:00:50.395
Earth sits at the center of the view.

00:00:50.395 --> 00:00:52.322
The Moon orbits around it.

00:00:52.322 --> 00:01:08.997
The spacecraft's arc — a translunar injection from low Earth orbit, cruise to lunar arrival, lunar orbit insertion — is drawn against Earth's reference frame, because everything important is happening in Earth's gravity well.

00:01:08.997 --> 00:01:16.260
Drawn heliocentric, this trajectory would be a tiny squiggle next to Earth's orbit around the Sun.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.113
You'd see nothing useful.

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:19.817
Both views are correct.

00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:27.154
The spacecraft is doing the same thing in both — gravity does not change with your choice of frame.

00:01:27.154 --> 00:01:29.304
But what you can see changes.

00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:33.380
A Mars mission needs heliocentric scale to be readable.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.604
A Moon mission needs Earth-centered scale to be readable.

00:01:37.604 --> 00:01:41.161
Use the toolbar to switch frames on any mission.

00:01:41.161 --> 00:01:43.755
Watch what becomes visible in each.

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:55.391
This is the same trick astronomers use when they switch between heliocentric, geocentric, and barycentric coordinates depending on what they want to compute.

00:01:55.391 --> 00:01:58.207
Choose the frame the problem lives in.

